        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue!\n\nThis is not an issue with 'mini.files' per se, but with the fact that 'snacks.picker' is closed if lost focus. Closing a 'mini.files' explorer returns focus to its target window, which also prompts 'snacks.picker' to close.\n\nTo see that it is not as broad of an issue as \"other floating windows are closed...\", instead of `:lua Snacks.picker.files()`, execute `:lua _G.open_win()` with its definition as something like this:\n\n```lua\n_G.open_win = function()\n  local config = { relative = 'editor', row = 0, col = vim.o.columns - 1, anchor = 'NE', height = 10, width = 10 }\n  local buf_id = vim.api.nvim_create_buf(false, true)\n  vim.api.nvim_open_win(buf_id, true, config)\nend\n```\n\nExecuting this will not close the newly created floating window and will keep it open+focused.\n\nIf anything, it might be an issue with 'snacks.picker' having too strict policy on window focus changing. When 'mini.files' loses focus, it temporarily sets target window as current and almost immediately returns focus to the window that was current when lost focus was detected. As it is done during the same loop, I'd say that this is an okay solution.\n\nAs 'mini.files' explorer is expected to be closed explicitly and as it is an issue with one specific plugin, I think I am going to close this as working as expected.",
